-# is really the builder weighs hundreds of megabytes.
+# Two stages, and the split is the whole design. The builder has the toolchain, the compiler's C
+# dependencies and the sources; the runtime has one binary, one unprivileged user and nothing that
+# could compile another one. What that buys is not disk space as much as surface: an image with no
+# `cargo`, no `rustc` and no sources is an image where a shipped vulnerability has fewer places to
+# live, and where "the image runs the binary this tree builds" is a claim somebody can check, because
