 ## The comparison
 
-<!-- TODO: the command (`scripts/repro.sh`), and then the block it prints, in a fenced block, line for
-line — the step's spec re-runs the command and compares that block, so it is edited from a run and
-never by hand. Say that the block is stdout and that both builds' progress goes to stderr, and that no
-digest, path or time is in it because those are the numbers that move. Name the two environment
-variables the script documents for the spec (`LAKELET_REPRO_ROOT`, `LAKELET_REPRO_KEEP`). -->
+```sh
+sh scripts/repro.sh
+```
 
 ```text
-toolchain=TODO
-verdict=TODO
+toolchain=1.98.1
+locked=true
+builds=2
+build_roots=differ
+target_roots=differ
+artifacts=lakeletd,lakeletq,lakelet-load,liblakelet.rlib
+artifacts_compared=4
+artifacts_identical=4
+artifacts_differing=0
+difference_path=remapped
+difference_timestamp=mtimes_differ
+verdict=reproducible
 ```
 
+The block is what the script prints on **stdout**, and nothing else is printed there: progress,
+including both `cargo build` runs, goes to stderr. That is not tidiness — the step's spec re-runs this
+script and compares the block line for line, so a line that moved between two machines would fail the
+step. Which is also why the block carries counts and names and never a digest, a path or a time: a
+digest of a binary changes the day a crate moves, and a page carrying one is a page that goes stale
+quietly. `docs/dr-drill.md` made the same trade for a restore's numbers and `docs/capacity.md` for a
+model's, and this is the third time the project has paid it.
+
+Two knobs exist for the spec rather than for a reader, and they are part of the script's interface:
+`LAKELET_REPRO_ROOT=<dir>` builds the two copies under `<dir>` instead of a temporary path, and
+`LAKELET_REPRO_KEEP=1` leaves them there. The step's spec sets both, then looks inside the trees at
+the artifacts the block says it compared — which is the difference between a script that compares
+files and a script that prints a verdict.
+
 ## The two differences a run can have, and what happened to each
 
-<!-- TODO: one subsection each. The path: what a compiler writes into what it produces, why the
-dependency rlibs' location is in the library's metadata, what `--remap-path-prefix` is passed over
-(the whole build location, in both builds), and how the script *checks* that no artifact still names
-it. The timestamp: the copies' modification times differ and are deliberately not compared, nothing in
-these artifacts records a time, and `SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH` is named so a reader knows it was considered.
-Then the toolchain: why the pin is a file and what the script does when the answering compiler is not
-the channel it names. -->
+Two builds of one tree can differ for two reasons. The script's block names both:
+
+**The path.** A build directory is not neutral. A compiler writes the names of the files it read into
+what it produces, and one of the files it read is the library it was linked against — which lives
+under the target directory. So two builds in two directories produce two artifacts whose metadata
+names two different places, at two different lengths, and everything that counts bytes afterwards
+moves with it. The mechanism is the standard one:
+[`--remap-path-prefix`](https://doc.rust-lang.org/rustc/command-line-arguments.html#--remap-path-prefix),
+passed by the script to **both** builds with the whole build location — the copy of the tree and the
+target directory beside it — renamed to the same `/build`. That is what `difference_path=remapped`
+means, and it is checked rather than asserted: before the block is printed, the script fails the run
+if any artifact still contains the directory it was built in. A remap over the sources alone is not
+enough, which is the trap this page exists to save somebody: `cargo` records where the *dependency
+rlibs* were, and those are under the target directory.
+
+The three binaries do not carry the location in a release build at all; the library's metadata does.
+That asymmetry is worth knowing before deciding the mechanism is unnecessary — `liblakelet.rlib` is
+the artifact this comparison would fail on without the flags, and a project that only compared its
+binaries would have a green block and an unexplained difference in the file its dependencies were
+compiled against.
+
+**The timestamp.** The two copies were written at different moments, so their modification times
+differ — that is `difference_timestamp=mtimes_differ`, and it is the difference the script
+deliberately does **not** remove. Nothing compares metadata here: the comparison is over bytes,
+because a modification time is a property of the copy and not of the artifact, and a reader who wants
+to know whether two builds are the same file has to look at content. What is worth stating plainly is
+that no timestamp is in these bytes to begin with: the archive members of an rlib carry a zero
+modification time (rustc writes them that way, so this is not a flag this project sets), and
+`SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH` — the standard lever for the tools that do stamp time into their output —
+changes nothing about this build. It is named here so that a reader who has met it elsewhere knows it
+was considered rather than missed.
+
+**The toolchain is the third input, and it is pinned rather than described.** A floating `stable` is
+a compiler that changes under a build that changed nothing, and two builds compared across that
+moment differ for a reason no flag removes — which is why the pin is a file in the tree and not a line
+in this page:
+[rust-toolchain.toml](../rust-toolchain.toml) names `1.98.1` and the two components the gates run, and
+the script refuses to build at all when the compiler answering is not the channel the file names. That
+refusal is the difference between "two builds agree" and "two builds agree *and* this machine is the
+one the file describes".
 
 ## What `--locked` and the lockfile buy
 
-<!-- TODO: `Cargo.lock` committed plus `--locked` on every build, what that makes impossible (a
-resolution this tree never saw), and the part it does *not* pin: the bytes of a fetched crate. Link
-`docs/supply-chain.md` for that half. -->
+[Cargo.lock](../Cargo.lock) is the other half of "the same inputs", and it is committed for one
+reason: with it, `cargo build` builds the versions this tree was tested with, and `--locked` — which
+the script passes and the pipeline passes — makes cargo refuse rather than quietly resolve something
+newer. Without a lockfile, "the same source" describes a tree whose dependencies are whatever the
+registry answers with that afternoon; the difference between the two is not academic, it is the
+difference between a build somebody can reproduce and a build somebody can only re-run.
+
+`--locked` is also what makes the comparison honest in a second sense: both builds resolve from the
+same file, so a difference between their bytes cannot be a difference between their dependency
+versions. The dependency *fetch* is the part the lockfile does not pin to bytes — the crates come from
+crates.io by name and version, not from a vendored copy in this repository, and `docs/supply-chain.md`
+names that gap rather than leaving the reader to find it.
 
 ## What the image changes
 
-<!-- TODO: two things — the path is different again inside the image (and nothing compares the image's
-binary with this host's by bytes), and the runtime image carries the binary rather than the compiler,
-which is what the 25 MB ceiling asserts. Then the part that is sharper than taste: no `LAKELET_*`
-variable is baked in, because `container-repro` compares the image's configuration report with this
-host's and passes the image's environment through only for `MAILER__` names. -->
+[Dockerfile](../Dockerfile) builds the same binary from the same sources, and the comparison above is
+the reason it can be trusted to: the build inside the image is the tree's own release build with
+`--locked`, and the `container-repro` gate then asks the binary inside the finished image the same
+question this machine's binary answers. The differences the image introduces are the interesting part
+and there are exactly two:
+
+- **the path is different, and different again.** The image builds in `/build`, so its artifacts
+  record that path; the host built in a directory under this project's parent. Nothing in the image's
+  build tries to hide that, and nothing compares the two builds byte for byte — comparing an image's
+  binary with a host binary by bytes would be comparing two *locations*, not two builds, which is the
+  whole subject of the section above;
+- **the runtime image carries the binary and not the compiler.** `alpine:3.20` plus one user plus
+  `/usr/local/bin/lakeletd`: no cargo, no rustc, no sources. The step's gate holds that stage to a
+  25 MB ceiling, and the ceiling is the assertion that the shipped thing is a runtime rather than a
+  build environment — the same claim, and the same number, that `mailer/1.21-container` makes for the
+  first project.
+
+What the image deliberately does **not** carry is configuration. It bakes in no `LAKELET_*` variable
+at all, not even the listen address a container needs, and the reason is sharper than taste: the
+`container-repro` gate compares the configuration report the image answers with the one this host's
+binary answers, and it passes the image's environment through to the host only for names in the
+`MAILER__` namespace (a leftover from the step that first built that gate for the first project). A
+`LAKELET_*` variable baked into the image is therefore a difference the gate reports as a failure. The
+listen address and the store directory arrive from the deployment — `LAKELET_LISTEN_ADDR` and
+`LAKELET_STORE_DIR`, which is what the four layers of `lakelet/4.1-ingest-protocol`'s configuration
+are for.
 
 ## When the image is wrong, and how to see it
 
-<!-- TODO: what `container-repro` does, its three ways to be red (the image does not build; the runtime
-stage is over 25 MB; the two configuration reports differ), and the drill one line long: point the
-runtime stage at the builder and read the size. -->
+The image has a gate of its own — `container-repro` — and it is worth knowing what it does before
+trusting it, because it is the only check in this project that builds the shipped artifact and then
+*asks it a question*. It builds the image the Dockerfile describes, holds the runtime stage to a 25 MB
+ceiling, and runs `--check-config` inside the image, comparing the answer line by line with the answer
+this machine's binary gives. Three ways to be red, and each names the decision that was wrong: the
+image does not build; the runtime stage is over 25 MB (which is what a stage that is really the
+builder weighs — hundreds of megabytes); or the two configuration reports differ, which means the
+binary in the image is not the binary this tree builds.
+
+So the drill is one line long, and it is the reason the ceiling is a number rather than a preference:
+point the runtime stage at the builder — `FROM ${BUILDER} AS runtime` — and the gate answers with the
+size, because a runtime stage that carries `cargo`, `rustc` and the sources is not a runtime. Put the
+line back and the gate is green again. A step's own page (`lakelet/4.22-reproducible-build`) carries
+the same drill as the thing a learner does before this page is finished; this paragraph is the same
+sentence for a reader of the project.
 
 ## What this does not achieve
 
-<!-- TODO: the three gaps, named the way `docs/dr-drill.md` and `docs/capacity.md` name theirs:
-bit-identical across machines is not measured here; the crates are fetched rather than vendored; and a
-build script that writes a date into a generated file would reintroduce a difference nothing here
-compares. -->
+Three things, named here so that nobody reads a green block as more than it is:
+
+- **bit-identical across machines.** The block is one machine comparing two directories. Two machines
+  differ in the compiler's build, the C library a `zstd-sys` build links against, the linker, and the
+  processor the code is compiled for; nothing here measures any of that. What is measured is what a
+  single machine can hold still — the same compiler, the same locked dependencies, two directories —
+  and the honest claim is the narrower one: *this tree builds into these bytes, twice, from two
+  places*;
+- **a vendored registry.** The crates are fetched, not stored in this repository, so a build with no
+  network needs a warm local registry cache, and the bytes of a crate are trusted to what crates.io
+  serves for that name and version rather than to a copy this project keeps. `cargo vendor` plus a
+  `[source]` replacement in `.cargo/config.toml` is the answer for a project that needs to build with
+  the network gone, and it is a decision about repository size that this step does not make;
+- **a build that never reads a clock.** `SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH` is honoured by toolchains that stamp time
+  into their output; this build's artifacts carry no timestamp to remove (see above), but a future
+  dependency with a build script that writes `date` into a generated file would reintroduce the
+  difference, and nothing here would catch it: the script compares the artifacts a build produces, not
+  the files a build reads. A build that must be reproducible on the strictest reading runs its build
+  scripts under a fixed date, and this project does not.
